 "music": "bed",
 "title": "Cache stampede",
 "sources": [
  "Wikipedia, 'Cache stampede' (secondary source): a cascading failure when a cached item expires under heavy load and many concurrent processes try to regenerate it at once; example: a page taking 3 seconds to render at 10 requests per second means 30 processes recomputing at once when the cached page expires; the extra load can exhaust shared resources such as databases, cause congestion collapse and keep the cache empty; mitigations: (1) locking: one process gets the lock and recomputes, the others wait, get not-found or serve a stale item, at the cost of an extra write and a more complex implementation; (2) external recomputation; (3) probabilistic early expiration, which costs extra cache memory for the stored recompute time. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cache_stampede",
  "Not claimed: Wikipedia does not discuss TTL jitter or request coalescing, so the video does not mention them. The 3 s / 10 req/s / 30 rebuilds numbers are Wikipedia's own example, shown as such."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Your most popular page is cached, so the database barely gets touched, until that cache entry expires.",
   "caps": "*POPULAR PAGE | CACHED *UNTIL IT EXPIRES"
  },
  {
   "text": "Now every request misses at the same moment, and each one goes to the database to rebuild it.",
   "caps": "*EVERY REQUEST MISSES | ALL *HIT THE DATABASE"
  },
  {
   "text": "A classic example: three seconds to build, ten requests per second, so thirty rebuilds at once.",
   "caps": "*3 SECONDS TO BUILD | *10 REQUESTS PER SECOND | *30 REBUILDS"
  },
  {
   "text": "All that load can overwhelm the database, which keeps the cache empty, and the whole thing collapses.",
   "caps": "DATABASE *OVERLOADED | CACHE STAYS *EMPTY | IT *COLLAPSES"
  },
  {
   "text": "The first fix is a lock, so only one request rebuilds, and the others wait or get a stale copy.",
   "caps": "*LOCK THE KEY | ONE *REBUILDS | OTHERS *WAIT"
  },
  {
   "text": "Another is early refresh, where a request randomly rebuilds the entry slightly before it expires.",
   "caps": "*REFRESH EARLY | A BIT *BEFORE EXPIRY"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: locks add an extra write and more complexity, and early refresh costs extra cache memory.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | LOCKS ADD *COMPLEXITY | EARLY REFRESH COSTS *MEMORY"
  },
  {
   "text": "So for a hot key, never let everyone rebuild together, let one request do the work.",
   "caps": "FOR A *HOT KEY | NEVER *REBUILD TOGETHER | LET *ONE DO THE WORK"
  }
 ],
